              ":tutorial/content": "Recall that a first-order linear ODE can always be expressed in the standard form\n\n$$\ny'(x)+P(x)y(x) =Q(x).\n$$\n\nFirst-order linear ODEs are special because we can always find the general solution using an **integrating factor.** \n\nAn integrating factor is a function $I(x)$ that allows us to rewrite the left-hand side as an exact derivative after multiplying the equation by $I(x).$ The resulting equation can then be solved by integrating both sides.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the following first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\frac{2}{x}\\, y = 3\n$$\n\nAs it stands, this differential equation cannot be integrated directly. However, if we multiply both sides of the equation by $I(x) = x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nx^2\\cdot \\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + x^2\\cdot \\frac{2}{x}\\, y = x^2\\cdot 3\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\nx^2 \\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + 2 x y = 3x^2 \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nNow, here's the neat part. Provided our integrating factor is correct, we can *always* write the left-hand side of the equation as the exact derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich in our case is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(y \\cdot x^2\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}( y \\cdot x^2) = 3x^2.\n$$\n\nWe can now find the general solution by integrating both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y\\cdot {x}^{2} & ={x}^{3}+C \\\\ y=x+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can check that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}( y \\cdot x^2) = 3x^2\n$$\n\nis indeed equivalent to the original equation $(\\ast)$ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)\\cdot {x}^{2}+y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 2x & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =3{x}^{2}\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy finding an appropriate integrating factor, we can solve any first-order linear ODE! So the question is, how do we find an integrating factor? Let's discuss this next.",
              ":tutorial/id": "1066a4d7-d790-5316-9925-1ac0e928ce01",
              ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 14550,
              ":tutorial/title": "Introduction"
            },
            ":step/id": "2aeafed5-8ce5-5873-95fe-baefd4b2fe7b",
            ":step/math-academy-id": 597598,
            ":step/next": {
              ":db/id": 17592186088818
            }
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186088818,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186088860,
              ":tutorial/content": "Given a first-order linear ODE, we can find an integrating factor and use it to solve the equation using the following procedure.\n\n- **Step 1**: Write the equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\n- **Step 2**: Compute an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition\n\n$$\nI(x) = e^{\\int P(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x} \\,.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3**: Multiply both sides of the equation found in step 1 by $I(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nI(x)\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + I(x)P(x)y = I(x)Q(x)\n$$\n\n- **Step 4**: Rewrite the left side as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y I(x)\\Bigr)\n$$\n\nIf steps 1 through 3 are done correctly, then we can always do this, and we can check that it's correct by differentiating. Our new equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y I(x)\\Bigr) = I(x)Q(x)\n$$\n\n- **Step 5**: Integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x$ and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int I(x)Q(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ yI(x) & =\\int I(x)Q(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{I(x)}(\\int I(x)Q(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nYou might be wondering where the formula for $I(x)$ comes from; we'll discuss this at the end of the lesson. First, let's apply this method to a concrete example in the next slide.",
              ":tutorial/id": "a6a381ed-e9ab-5deb-9d74-30900610edf1",
              ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 14551,
              ":tutorial/title": "The Integrating Factor Method"
            },
            ":step/id": "552b03ae-2839-592c-a5fa-667890639337",
            ":step/math-academy-id": 597599,
